mbedding climate resilience into national infrastructure development. "),x(),w(6,"p"),A(7," \u201cHistorically, PWD relied on historical climate data for infrastructure design. However, through the Van KIRAP project, future-focused climate projections - including rainfall patterns, coastal inundation risks, and temperature changes - are now standard inputs in all new and replacement infrastructure projects. This shift ensures that Vanuatu\u2019s roads, buildings, and public utilities are designed to withstand future climate challenges, rather than just past conditions. "),x(),w(8,"p"),A(9,' A major test of this approach came in December 2024, when a powerful earthquake caused widespread damage to roads and infrastructure. Thanks to the integration of climate-informed design, the rebuilding process prioritized "build back better" principles, ensuring that reconstructed infrastructure is more resilient to both seismic activity and climate impacts. '),x(),w(10,"p"),A(11," Additionally, the infrastructure sector played a key role in aerial assessments using drone technology as part of the Van KIRAP project. These assessments provided critical pre- and post-disaster data, helping guide rapid response efforts and long-term reconstruction planning. This success has spurred new projects aimed at expanding aerial mapping capabilities to further strengthen disaster preparedness and infrastructure resilience.\u201d "),x(),w(12,"p"),A(13," The progress made between 2022 and 2024 marks a turning point for infrastructure development in Vanuatu. By embedding climate information into design and practice, the Public Works Department has set a new standard - one where resilience is no longer an option, but a necessity for sustainable development. "),x(),w(14,"p"),A(15," - Raviky Talae, Infrastructure Sector Coordinator, Van-KIRAP (Public Works Department) "),x(),w(16,"div",2),de(17,"img",3),x()()())},styles:["h1[_ngcontent-%COMP%], h2[_ngcontent-%COMP%], h3[_ngcontent-%COMP%]{font-st